A||||48||Carolina 88GT||Sprint Headers||||5||
Z||000000||Carolina 88GT||02-15-2000||01:10 PM||||Was wondering of anyone had the sprint manifolds from "The Fiero Store" installed a long with the oclet exhaust.<P>Was wanting to change em out, but want advice on this or if there is a better setup out there.||||reg||5
Z||000001||88 Fiero||02-15-2000||02:17 PM||||Don't buy it...waste of money...you don't gain anything at all....or if you want..asked them if they could provide you with a dyno-run with this headers on and see if there is any gain...||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||02-15-2000||02:34 PM||||I have a set of Sprint Headers installed on my '86 SE and can tell you that the difference from stock is a very noticable improvement. I do not have the Ocelot exhaust system but I do have a less restrictive Catalytic converter and a performance muffler. Going to the performance cat/muffler was a slightly noticable improvement. I did notice a bigger difference between the stock manifolds and the Sprint headers. I do feel the headers are expensive, But worth it in the long run.<BR>(no more cracks, free flow design, and look good too) The other thing I like about the headers is it is a direct bolt-on to the exsisting exhaust system. I installed them myself and typed an article for our local Fiero club with the results. For more details see: <A HREF="http://home.xnet.com/~nife/articles/tt-sprintheaders.html" TARGET=_blank>http://home.xnet.com/~nife/articles/tt-sprintheaders.html</A> <P>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 02-15-2000).]<p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 02-15-2000).]||||reg||10
Z||000003||88formula||02-16-2000||12:28 PM||||How do those headers compare to just porting the stock manifolds?||||reg||1
Z||000004||Oreif||02-16-2000||02:16 PM||||The sprint headers have "tuned" lengths where the stock manifolds do not. I have not tried the removal of the resrtictors in the stock manifolds and at present do not know anyone who has done it. So I don't really know how it compares to the headers.||||reg||1
Z||000005||cobra427||02-16-2000||03:51 PM||||Orief, I kindly take issue with the statement<BR>that the sprint headers are a "tuned" header system. Correct me if I'm wrong but I was always under the impression that a tuned header was of equal measured length of pipe that met at a collector. The collector I'll give you but the tuned lenght, here is where we part company. They,(s/headers)may flow a little better than the non relieved stock header, but thats as far as I would go.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Carolina 88GT||02-16-2000||04:12 PM||||Cobra...so in your opinion, the difference between the stock and "upgrade" would not justify the 500 or so bucks.  Unless of course if the manifold was cracked and needed replaced anyways.||||reg||5
Z||000007||Raydar||02-16-2000||04:53 PM||||I have heard the welded and ported stock manifolds will flow *nearly* as well as the Sprint manifolds. (I was previously chastised for calling them "headers". And I don't think the Fiero Store uses that term, any more.) But the stockers are not *nearly* as sexy looking.||||reg||1
Z||000008||superXmotorsports||02-16-2000||06:37 PM||||I'm about to try (soon I hope) Fiero Heavens B&B designed headers.  They are equal length and bolt to the factory downpipe.  I don't have their dyno #'s yet but I will post mine after they are installed.  <P>I don't care for the Ocelot exhaust.  No offense to those who do.  It's not really any more free flowing than the factory system, the tips look wimpy, and it has a bad resonation at about 3,000RPM's.  I think the ultimate setup would be both the B&B headers and the all-polished B&B exhaust.  If anyone has trouble reaching Fieroheaven let me know and I'll contact them for you.  <BR> <A HREF="http://www.fieroheaven.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroheaven.com</A> <P>SXM.||||reg||2
Z||000009||DXR_DAD||02-16-2000||07:45 PM||||FS= Sprint Performance "MANIFOLDS" Note: it does not say header?,  "Enhances flow and increases life and durability"  Cobra you hit the nail on the head A true "Tuned Header".<P>Carolina, I did the port and re-weld on a NEW set of stock manifolds for less than $500.00 I have no dyno test on my car but I'm happy with the gain that I can fell. I've been told 8-15hp gain fron this mod? To each his own, but for the price? you know your own pockets$ If the stock manifolds last 12yrs. before cracking I think another 12yrs is ok with better flow that might prevent cracking? <P>Just my 00.02||||reg||5
Z||000010||Carolina 88GT||02-16-2000||08:53 PM||||DXR-Dad....never thought of buying a new set of stock manifolds and portin' them.  But you still have to have the heat shields installed?  I liked the idea that you could remove those with the sprint manifolds......again though is that really worth the 500 bucks....from what Im reading.....No.||||reg||1
Z||000011||cobra427||02-16-2000||09:11 PM||||C/88g.here is my opinion on the header situation.Raydar makes a good point for the FS manifolds. They do look rather nice.$500.00 is a lot of money to look good for little gain.<BR>Fiero Heaven has yet to have anything concrete yet and no telling when thats going to be. Hopefully soon,I have been following their site since last Sept. and I don't see much progress yet.Can't wait to see the dyno results on them as well.  Jury is still out on them as far as I'm concerned. Around 5 bills for them too.<BR>Chris West has headers.Have not seen any #'s on their pipes,Also around 5 bills.<BR>FOCOA sells headers too. About the same price. They do look nice as well but they to have no #'s. <BR>So for now,until someone comes up with some justifiable numbers, I'll stick with the ported factory jobbies for now. <BR>    Just my humble opinion besides I'm still trying to figure out how to get a handle on my twin turbo setup.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Carolina 88GT||02-16-2000||09:38 PM||||Cobra...good points.  besides, ...I can wait and pocket the 5 bills.  Maybe a less restrictive cat/muffler would be a better bet right now.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Oreif||02-17-2000||01:40 AM||||Cobra, I know what you mean by equal length. But that is provided the collector at the end is 2 times that of the individual runner. The sprint headers are not collectively tuned the are stagger tuned.  The lengths or the runners are staggered (tuned) for a specific flow pattern. This is done because it uses the exsisting exhaust system which includes the Y-pipe which is a stock size. The best set-up would be a dual<BR>exhaust system with the header runners being equal length. Like I said before I do not know the flow differences between the stock manifolds (ported) and the sprint headers. I do know that when I bought mine 3 years ago FS listed them at $437.00 and when you order from FS and give them a valid Fiero Club membership ID they take off 10%. So I got the headers for under $400. I do agree that they are still expensive even at that price.<BR>I am satisfied with the performance gain I got from them. If you ever find #'s for the future headers let me know.<p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 02-17-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||cobra427||02-17-2000||05:44 PM||||Orief, <BR>I find that thos are a very interesting choise of words."Stagger Tuned". I've been around this "car thingy" for about 45 years and I can't say that I've ever heard that used to discribe exhaust mechanics.Live and learn I guess.<BR>Now as far as the price of the F/S manifolds go,my earliest catalog goes back to 1993 and they weren't even listed at that point in time. My next News letter from the F/S was the Spring issue of 1997.In there it list the Sprint Manifolds, part# 95727(same as they are today)with the price of $549.00.(Again the same price today) If you were to use your 10% discount,that still makes them $494.10.(still in the neighborhood of $500.00.  The F/S to the best of my knowledge never offered a discount for any items.However, you may have be a little confused as to the company that did offer a 10% discount. It was IRM that offered a 10% discount to any recognised club<P>One more thought about the F/S manifolds. What their earlier claims were all about was what they coined as "proportional flowing".<BR>Simply stated it was"to reduce the restriction by massaging and smoothing out the flow of exhaust through the COLLECTOR PIPES". Taken from their ad.<BR>I hope that you do not take this as a flame. It is not mean to be. Just trying to keep the facts straight. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||7
Z||000015||Carolina 88GT||02-17-2000||06:02 PM||||Any of you here anything about the "Flow-well Exhaust Headers", and I will use the term they did so as not to incite a riot *ha from West Coast Fiero?  I would like see some of the "customer testomonials"||||reg||7
Z||000016||cobra427||02-17-2000||06:49 PM||||C/88GT, thos are the ones from Chris West I was talking about. Around $500 with no dyno figures. You make the call.||||reg||1
Z||000017||Oreif||02-17-2000||08:13 PM||||Cobra, I don't take it as a flame. But the Headers were available in '95 If you ordered a catalog it was in a flyer, it wasn't listed in the catalog. If I remember correctly the flyer was an ad from the company that made them I think they were in England or it was designed there. (it's been awhile) but I do know that the original price was $437. (pause) I looked thru my stuff and cannot find the flyer for the exact spec of the headers but on the price sheet dated "effective OCT 95" It shows "List price" is $529 "Our Price" as $437. As for the 10% I know the Fiero Store offers it at least for our club because I've ordered from them for the last 5 years and have always received the discount. The only thing I've ever purchased from IRM was my Indy Scoop. After that experience I'll always look for another place to buy stuff other than IRM, But that's another story. Also back then F/S was the only place I heard about selling headers. The term Stagger tuned exhaust is a racing boat term.<BR>I beleive your wording is what the header ad said, But the headers look exactly like the ones used in boat racing. Hence the term stagger tuned.<p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 02-17-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000018||Shaun41178||02-17-2000||11:02 PM||||I call B.S on anyone who says they could feel a difference with the ported stock manifolds or the Fiero Store exhaust manifold system.  I ported out my stock exhaust manifold system and then had it dynoed and I picked up 8 hp but lost a few torque at the same time.  Did i feel this difference?  Hell no and you won't either.  8 hp is nothing.  You will only feel a 20 hp gain or more based on a stock motor.  This was on a relatively new engine(approx 25-30k) miles at the time so this engine had little to no blowby.  For those engines that have higher miles on them you will pick up less.  As far as the Fiero Store manifolds go I would guess between a 8-12 hp gain.  However this is only a guess and untill someone actually has them dynoed tested we can only guess.  I will have them dynoed if someone is willing to offer to let me borrow the Fiero Store manifolds for a bit.  It seems to me that most to all people on this list aren't too concerned with true increased performance.  Most say they can feel a difference and that it makes a difference but none except me and a few others are willing to put up a bit of cash($35) to have the car dynoed.  I find it funny how you guys can spend $500+ on a exhaust upgrade and then not go out and see how much more hp you picked up or lost.  Everyone seems to have a super accurate "ass-meter" to measure increases in hp.  HAHAHAHA you guys make me laugh.<P>I applaud SXM for taking some initiative and having the Fiero Heaven exhaust dynoed.  So far I have dynoed a ported throttle body, underdrive pulley, ported stock exhaust manifolds, different intake system, and nitrous.  1fst2m6 has dynoed a few bolt on parts too and I applaud him as well.  To everyone else who says they can feel a difference with any of the mods above excluding the nitrous, I scoff at you.||||reg||8
Z||000019||mwbackus||02-17-2000||11:21 PM||||Gee.. nice attitude!! NOT!! There are several ways to determine changes in performance; g-tech makes a nice instrument, and 1/4 mile times are also a good indicator.  If you are so much better than everyone else here, why do you even hang around??||||reg||1
Z||000020||Oreif||02-17-2000||11:31 PM||||Shaun, I'm not saying that I can "feel" a horsepower change I just said I can feel a difference. The difference being the response feels better, My idle is stable, all around drivability is better and my gas milage is about 1% better. I don't have to have my car dyno'd to tell me it runs better with a less restrictive exhaust system. You say you could not feel a difference, maybe you should pay attention to how your car is running instead of numbers. I'm not counting Horsepower and I'm not looking to race the "brag queens"<BR>If horsepower was a primary concern I'd just get a 3.8L supercharged or a V-8. My only goal is to keep my car running very well. So scoff all you want. If you want to flame people and cause discontent, maybe you should seek another forum. Now it's my turn to scoff at you!<p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 02-17-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000021||cobra427||02-17-2000||11:34 PM||||shaun41178, I can see that you are a guy that oozes with tact. Why don't you really tell us how you feel.Please don't hold it in.<P>Don't you think that you could have made your point in a little kindlier manner? I say to your statements SO WHAT! All of us tell a few war stories and puff a little. If you find something that is grossly wrong,feel free to make corrections but don't chop off our heads in the process.<P>I would suggest,that you owe the forum an appology.  Just a thought.<p>[This message has been edited by cobra427 (edited 02-18-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000022||Phaeton||02-18-2000||01:37 AM||||I replaced my cat with a straight pipe, I could swear it revved quicker and accelerated faster, so much so the clutch burnt out immediately.  I felt that in the seat of my pants, no scoff.||||reg||1
Z||000023||Phil||02-18-2000||06:51 AM||||Definately the weather!||||reg||1
Z||000024||Tom88gt||02-18-2000||02:09 PM||||Cobra is right.  <P>A properly designed header must have equal length tubes.  The exhaust needs to be evenly spaced out so it reaches the collector at even intervals in order to minimize turbulence and achieve a smooth flow.  The firing order is 123456 so 135 and 246 must have equal length tubes.  Why else would racers go to extreme lengths to build equal length headers?  <P>Have you ever seen Hall Pantera's or Ford GT-40 headers?  They are not at all convinient to work on or install but they illustrate the importance placed upon equal length tubes.  <P>Many header designs do not do this but must compromise for fit.  And the Fiero is a hard fit.  It doesn't mean they are wrong, just not ideal.<P>SXM...can you tell us more about the equal length headers you found?||||reg||12
Z||000025||DRH||02-18-2000||02:11 PM||||Shaun,<P>Your statement would imply that if something went wrong and your car <B>suddenly</B> started making 15 <B>LESS</B> horsepower you wouldn't notice. I find that hard to believe.<P>David.||||reg||1
Z||000026||88formula||02-18-2000||07:34 PM||||Holly cow, It was just a simple question. I think the ported STOCK exhaust manifolds would be the best bang for the buck if PISA Corp can make a 190 horsepower stroked 2.8 with them. See for yourself, <A HREF="http://www.cybercars.com/mnfr/pisa3.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.cybercars.com/mnfr/pisa3.htm</A>  <P><p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 02-18-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000027||88 Fiero||02-18-2000||07:47 PM||||FOCOA's Headers are of equal length... I know that it will produce at least 25% horsepower. Fiero Doc of Fiero Motorsports have his Orange Fiero Dynoed with and without the headers and there was an increase in Horsepower. Also, the guy that do their Headers is the same guy that does the Headers for the Buick Grand National Cars. And I heard alot about this guy...He knows his shit and it comes to Headers...||||reg||1
Z||000028||stsmithgt||02-19-2000||03:15 PM||||Nothing like a little scoffing to spice things up. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000029||Shaun41178||02-19-2000||03:50 PM||||<BR>mwbackus:  I have a g-tech and know what they can do and how accurate they are.  When it comes to measuring hp there are too many factors that affect the accuracy of the g-tech.  One of them being the road.  You need a perfectly flat road.  Good luck finding one of those.  Even the smallest imperfections will cause the g-tech to be off.  Next is vehicle weight.  That has to be correct for the g-tech to get a accurate reading.  If you are as much as 10 pounds off then the g-tech won't give an accurate number.  When I g-teched my underdrive pulley my g-tech read a loss of 1 hp but when I went to a chassis dyno it read a +4 gain in hp.<P>Oreif:  I too felt a difference in throttle response and my engine reved cleaner too after porting out my stock exhaust manifolds but I could not feel a difference in hp.  I went to a dyno to have it checked and I picked up +8 hp peak with it but lost torque at the same time.  And yes you are right you don't need a dyno to tell you your car is running better.  I find it funny how you made the statement, <BR>"You say you could not feel a difference, maybe you should pay attention to how your car is running instead of numbers."<BR>Well you might not know this but I run nitrous through my engine so I have to pay attention to how it runs.  One way of doing that is taking my car to a dyno to see how it is running and to check for changes in performance from different bolt ons.  If the numbers start dropping then I know my car is not running like it should so I diagnose the problem and make the appropriate modifications to correct it.  Numbers don't lie<P>I am sure after putting the Fiero Store manifolds on someone has seen them and asked how much hp you picked up with them. I can almost guarantee that at one point or another you have told someone around 8-10 hp increase or maybe you even told them more then that.  If you have never had your car dynoed then how would you know how much you picked up exactly?  You could have even lost hp and torque(although doubtfull).  Now if you have never made then statement then I apologize but I am pretty certain you have.<P>I find it funny how you scoff at me for wanting to know the "true" increase in performance from mods that I make to my car instead of it just "feeling" better or faster then before.  I like to have concrete numbers and numbers don't lie.  So if you want to scoff at me for having concrete numbers then go ahead I guess.  It doesn't bother me.  But I can tell you this.  EVERYONE on this forum would much rather have concrete numbers of performance improvements then just a feeling of something being better.  Cause if noone has concrete evidence such as dyno numbers then the same questions keep coming up like how much hp will an underdrive pulley add and everyone will be guessing without trully knowing.<P>DRH:  At what rpm would this 15 hp loss be at and would this loss be at WOT?  I will tell you that I would not feel a difference.  15 hp is equivalant to .15 in the qtr mile.  No one is capable of differentiating between a 16.0 run and a 16.15.  In fact I can't tell the difference between a 14.2 run and a 14.6 run.  I don't care how accurate you think your butt meter is you won't be able to tell.  The only thing you will be able to say is, "Well it felt faster"  But will it trully be faster?  I have told myself that once.  I have told myself that one run felt faster then the previous but in actuallity I ran .2 slower then the previous run and I said to myself it "felt" faster.  But how could that be when I told myself it felt faster?  Its because butt-meters aren't accurate.  Numbers don't lie.<P>88 fiero:  a 25% increase huh?  I am guessing you mean on a stock engine?  If so that is a 35 hp increase!!!!  I will try to be tact when saying this but I don't think so.  When you saw the dyno numbers the hp went up 35 hp peak?  Man if I made headers that made 35 hp and then sold them for $550 I wouldn't be able to keep them on the shelf.  And that is "at least 25%" according to you so that means a minimum of 35 hp which means it could be like 40 hp or even 45 hp.  Which means that for $550 you could have a 180 hp motor.  Yea like I said a 25% increase is way optimistic.  I would say a 15-20 hp gain at the most.  I am basing this statement on the fast that youcan get roughly 8 frmported stock ones and the better flow of the Focoa ones and the crossover pipe could be worth another 7 hp.  I used to be a Focoa member and in the magazine it said "dyno tested, hp increaeses".  It says nothing about how much was gained.  If it was actually 35 hp then they would have said 35 hp cause that is something to brag about.<P>In my opinion the stock manifolds ported are good enough for a stock or slightly modified 2.8.  When you start going to a 3.2 or 3.4L engines then they just become too restrictive in the upper rpms.  I would either go with the Focoa ones or West Coast Fiero exhaust system or even the Fiero Heaven ones for ture gains in hp and torque with a modified engine.  If you have a stock to mild 2.8 and don't want to port the stocker manifolds, go with the Fiero Store manifolds.||||reg||7
Z||000030||mwbackus||02-19-2000||04:03 PM||||Maybe you can use your G-tech to measure the speed of the BS that oozes from you mouth whenever you open it.||||reg||1
Z||000031||Cliff Pennock||02-19-2000||04:32 PM||||Ehm... Guys?||||reg||4
Z||000032||Phil||02-19-2000||04:45 PM||||Just remember folks there is a bit of difference twixt flywheel HP and rear wheel HP. Manufacturers almost always quote flywheel HP gains because the numbers are larger. 10HP flywheel gain will yield about 8 at the wheels so which would sound better in an ad 8 or 10? Then there is the ole "up to XXHP gain"  easy to do if you're starting with a 300HP engine. Granted numbers don't lie but they sure can be misleading.<BR> Shaun is in my opinion doing his mods in a logical fashion by getting dyno # after each change, tact maybe could use some help but what the hey. I know my car feels different each day, definately feels faster after it gets washed and waxed(must be cleaner air flow). I shall now follow the advise of an old wise man that once said "never pass up an opportunity to shut up" so bye now.<p>[This message has been edited by Phil (edited 02-19-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000033||GT Bastard||02-19-2000||05:17 PM||||I'm not sure about everyone else, but this topic is cracking me up!   ||||reg||1
Z||000034||Phaeton||02-19-2000||06:23 PM||||OK Fine then, I confess.  My clutch burned up because I just love the sound of downshifting through the gears.  I made the joke because this is funny.<BR>My ass-meter hasn't been calibrated lately, but I trust its accuracy on an emotional level separate from the area that checks the rockwell # on a heat treated rod.<BR>Vroom vroom bwaaaaaaaaaaaa   dang but I'm fast!  I got bow-tie exhaust tips and red rims, and my imron paint makes my Fiero even faster (how much faster I won't say).<BR>And if anyone tells me that grinding down the valve guide inside the exhaust port actually costs 2% on airflow....I know its pretty and polished in there and that pushes my butt meter up enough to more than make up for for the loss or real world power.<BR>When I raced I raced by the numbers.  I drive the Fiero by the seat of my pants.<BR>But don't stop doing dyno's and telling us the results, tact not neccessary, I do enjoy the info.  Often to distort it enough to fit my Fiero when I'm telling power lies.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000035||batboy||02-19-2000||08:07 PM||||I don't think Shaun is trying to B.S. anyone, he's just stating some high performance facts that he's learned and also mentioned a few personal opinions, which we all have a right to do. I appreciate the info that Shaun is providing us and hope he continues to do so. For the most part, I agreed with most everything he said, but like some of the others, I cringed at the way he said it. So what! Being tactful is not one of my strong points either, but I can often get away with it by claiming to be old and grumpy. Let's not resort to name calling and nitpicking just because the guy didn't talk to you in the same way that <B>you</B> "have" to talk to your wife and/or girlfriend.<P>I agree we want the most accurate info possible, but let's not squabble amongst ourselves just because you "think" someone is copping an attitude. Stating why you disagree and presenting evidence to support this is good enough. No reason to get into any pissing matches in the process. Perhaps we are all a little sensitive lately due to some juvenile smoldering flame battles that have been plaguing the forum lately. Nuff said.<P>Shaun, one thing I do disagree with is your statement that dyno numbers don't lie. Well... they don't always tell the whole truth. Dynos do have to be calibrated and corrected for temperature, air pressure, etc. There is a possibility for a small amount of error to creep in when doing dyno testing. If you are using the same dynometer each time and the same guy is doing the calibration, then you get better consistency in your results. Phil pointed out the net vs. gross horsepower thing too.<P>Many people seem to forget about torque when they talk about power. High horsepower numbers are good only in the upper RPM range (top end). Torque determines how quick you go from a dead stop to that upper RPM range where horsepower takes over. Give me a big wide flat torque curve throughout the entire midrange and I'll be happy.<P>Fiero owners have gotten a raw deal with ourcramped engine compartments and lack of reasonably priced headers. Truly, increasing the engine's breathing capacity is a sure way to increase power, regardless of the expense.||||reg||1
Z||000036||Shaun41178||02-19-2000||09:10 PM||||batboy:  Thank you and you're right, I am not trying to BS anyone.  And to clarify, all my dyno runs were done on the same dyno, by the same technician(he's the only one) and all were SAE corrected for temperature and humidity.<P>Basically I am just tired of everyone talking about how much adding this or taking away that from the engine or modifying it somehow will add a quoted amount of horsepower and or torque but noone has dyno tests to back up the statements.  So I have gone out and dynoed these parts and mods to see what they would do cause I myself was curious.  Then I give numbers and I am a BSer.  Yea I guess I could have said it a bit nicer but like I said I am tired of everyone talking like they know what they are talking about but not really knowing.  I don't know maybe i am going through withdrawal from the dragstrip and all this snow not allowing me to go out on the weekends.<P>Mwbackus: I think in order to measure the speed of the ooze coming out of my mouth, my g-tech would not have the capabilities of of measureing such speed.  For one thing it is not equiped with a radar gun so measuring the speed would be impossible with the g-tech.  i would need the proper device.  If the g-tech did have such a provision then i still wouldn't be able to get a perfectly accurate reading cause it would be difficult for me to keep perfectly parellel to the ground and to also keep the projecting B.S parellel to the ground.  Perhaps in the future technology will improve enough for me to get a measurement.<P>Don't worry Cliff I will not stoop to name calling levels and flames and keep it proffesional <P>Phaeton: your first post cracked me up but the second one was even better.  i enjoyed reading that especially the end and the "power lies" hehe  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"><P>||||reg||1
Z||000037||Shaun41178||02-19-2000||10:52 PM||||ok that was supposed to be a smilie face.  I guess they have changes on me.  Which keys are for teh smilie now?||||reg||1
Z||000038||BBBAD GT||02-20-2000||11:56 AM||||I don't want to start anything here, but I've been round and round with other BBS boards about dynos.  Yes, they are a great tool, but in my opinion, the dragstrip is where it all matters.  I've seen so many 'dyno-queens' that couldn't get out of their own way at the strip, and you've all heard the quote "you can't race a dyno".  <P>If you are evaluating a product, I'd agree a dyno would be more accurate and consistant because of factors that could be present in differnt days of drag strip testing (ie weather, driver, etc...).  <P>Lately, I've been preaching about how a dyno could be giving guys who are on the edge of their fuel system a false impression of hope.  Example:  Most 5.0 guys with a built motor and blowers (these are common) are still running stock lines and fuel rails.  They post 450-500 rwhp and the a/f ratio appears to be good enough to not warrant better fuel systems.  Only for them to find out at the first drag strip pass that they blow a gasket or even a hole in the piston.  Why?  Because on the strip (or extened periods on the street), you have a higher demand on the fuel system from all the gears than just 4th gear on the dyno.  <P>I've personally have never been on a dyno or hooked up ana/f ratio, I've just done it on my own and been erroring on the 'safe' side.  <P> <A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/" TARGET=_blank>http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/</A> <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000039||Raydar||02-20-2000||04:52 PM||||Ass-meter? Sounds like something from Southpark. (Anybody got any Cheesy-Poofs?)||||reg||1
Z||000040||Pretender||02-23-2000||01:26 AM||||3hp? i went to fiero heaven and if i read the chart correctly they only give 3hp? i am planning on buying a set of headers as soon as my income tax gets back so which should i buy??? fiero store sprint manifolds? fiero heaven headers?...anymore on the market? plase tell me! thanks in advance...shawn.||||reg||1
Z||000041||penetrator||02-23-2000||05:23 AM||||I only know one thing for sure...<P>BEFORE i revmoved my Catalysator, plastic thing in the air intake, another exhaust(that was, was it said, better than stock), repaced all ignintion parts and installed Theeee Fierostore Sprint Manifolds, i could not even compare my car with my fathers Camaro LT1(3-speed automatic)-78(with real headers). But now, after all changes i almost wins when we go from 0-100Km/h (62mph). It's just a matter of maybe 0.5 seconds. I dont know what changes who did what. And i dont have any figures about 1/4mile or 0-60 or any dyno tests. But im going to do a dyno test this summer...And I did feel the difference before and after.  Thats all folks!!!  :-)||||reg||1
Z||000042||1FST2M6||02-24-2000||04:17 PM||||CRAP I've been out of the loop for a few days and all this goes down... need to start paying more attention.. Any who. Everything that I have ever done to my litte rocket has been done one piece at a time and dynoed So i could show a gain or loss just like Shaun. I have gone to the race track on friday night and turned a TERRIBLE 14.7 take it to the shop have it "Dyno Tuned" messing with fuel map, timing, O2 sensor issues, and a host of other things the very next night within 3 degrees farenheit (sp) of the night before and lay down a 13.6@103(both runs made with full bottles at about the same temerature). I've spoken with LOADS of people and after dyno tuning your car may actually feel slower. Just cause its running smoother. I too have found this to be true. Cause my fastest 1/4 time was with my girlfriend in the car with a video tape rolling... Still can't explain that... I was pulling away from a POS 92 automatic vette and just seemed to take forever but when i got to the box and the time slip printed I about puked! (July 30, 99 Atlanta International Dragway) Seat of the pants is what most people use and thats fine. But if I can't justify a purchase with actual proof I won't keep it. I can't afford to throw parts at my car and think its faster. But the dyno can do strange things... (personal experiance) I've seen a kid put his car on the Dyno just for a baseline run, to see what he could lay down. After the run he pulled his car out and left, about 20 minutes later he returned swearing that his car was "..So much freekin' faster..." The tech didn't touch a thing! hooked the spark lead up strapped it down, I ran it up through the gears twice and a high speed run and that was it. All Psychological but it worked for him... he used his (booty-o-meter) No real point to my story just saying Dyno has its place in the performance world and the booty-o-meter does too, Just use whatever is more accessable to you but remember, both work and both need to be calibrated every once in a while.  Jus my $.02 oh... and everyone go by a nitrous kit.. that will destroy every little bit of botty calibrating you've ever done!||||reg||1
Z||000043||WKDFIRO||02-25-2000||01:25 AM||||Actually FOCOA headers don't sit on the shelf for very long at all. So if someone does go to a dyno and get results you scoff at them anyway? <P>What gives?<P>BTW I think it was the "HAHAHAHA" part of your tirade that set the flames a fannin'. Nobody likes to be laughed at.||||reg||1
Z||000044||Phaeton||02-25-2000||03:50 AM||||Well I think it was the "I scoff at you" part. Personally I don't mind making him laugh.<P>Butt meter calibration:<BR>One highly modified Olds Vista Cruiser 9 passenger stationwagon and one stock 88GT. <BR>Drive the wagon, drive the Fiero, drive the wagon, drive the Fiero.<BR>It was very obvious the Fiero could kick the carp out of the wagon, the difference in acceleration was not even close, the Fiero set me back in the seat hard.<BR>Yep, that's right, I could only read the "Oldsmobile" badge for a very short time before it got out of range.<P>My ex kept the wagon and left me the kids and the Fiero, a fair trade but sometimes right after being cut off by some twerp in a camaro I really miss that wagon.  Instead of just laughing at his manners, the wagon allowed me to scoff at his performance.<P>So I scoff at the HAHAHAHA.<P>It's late, does any of this make sense or is a flamer about scoffing vs. laughing going to start.<P>What? Huh? This is the technical discussion & questions forum? <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"> <P>Oh, sorry, never mind the above. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000045||Shaun41178||02-25-2000||03:56 PM||||WKDFiro:  Do you work at Focoa with Fiero Phil?  Do you talk to him often?  If the answer is no to either of those questions then how do you know that the Focoa Manifolds don't sit on the shelf long?<P>You said:  " So if someone does go to a dyno and get results you scoff at them anyway?"<P>You still don't get it.  I am not scoffing at the people that go and have mods dynoed to find true hp gains or losses.  Cause if that was true then i would be scoffing at me.  I scoff/laugh at the people who think that by adding a K&N filter to their car they will feel a difference and pick up 20 hp like the adds claim.  I just had a guy on Peters list say that he added a MSD coil and he could feel a difference in acceleration and that his car was faster. That was the only mod he did too. Yea right.  That is the person I scoff at.  If they want to believe that then fine but I will tell them the truth and if they decide not to believe me then oh well.<P>If someone went and had the Focoa Manifolds dyno tested and gave me numbers and even better yet a copy of the dyno printout and it said after the only mod being the manifols gave the car a minimum 35 hp on a stock internal engine then I would be happy for them and probably get a pair myself.  That reminds me that there is a website on the net somewhere of a guy that dyno tested the FOCOA Manifolds and only got like 15 hp and that was with a few other mods so it wasn't determined just how much the Manifolds added by themselves.  If someone remembers the site I am talking about then put the link up here for all to go to and find out for themselves.<P>Phaeton:  I am not talking about butt calibrations between two different cars cause that is too easy.  Sure I could get into a 99 Firebird and be able to tell that it is faster then my car plus having prior knowledge of the cars capabilities helps with that decision as well.  But we are talking about comparing the same car to itself prior to a mod and after the mod.<P>Be as upset with me at my original post with the laughing and the "scoffing" but I stand by it 100%||||reg||1
Z||000046||DRH||02-25-2000||07:06 PM||||Shaun, <P>In your comment about the guy with the MSD coil aren't you doing exactly what you are accusing the "non-dyno" people of doing? What I mean by that is you are arriving at a conclusion without any hard evidence to back it up. Your statement that he could not feel the difference is "seat of the pants". <P>For all we know his old coil was almost dead. In that case, your statement that he could not feel the difference could very well be incorrect. <P>To get the equivalent of your dyno testing level of confidence we would need to perform a "blind" test with the guy. Have him drive the car several times with someone else randomly switching the coils. Have him report his "feel" and compare to reality.<P>Not trying to start a war here... just something to think about...<P>Carolina 88GT, In response to your original question.. I'm sorry, but I don't have any experience with sprint headers.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>||||reg||5
Z||000047||88 Fiero||02-25-2000||07:41 PM||||Shaun..didn't you say your car is automatic...well..the gear is so much different than having a 4-speed with a 4.10 gear or even the getrag 5-speed. granted that all the mods that you've done on your car and you have a 4-speed or 5-speed fiero...I bet ya..you'll notice a difference even with headers...so not making any war here...it's really hard to justify speed acceleration with automatic vs. a stick shift..unless you have a V8 engine in it...||||reg||1
Z||000048||88 Fiero||02-25-2000||07:43 PM||||Shaun..didn't you say your car is automatic...well..the gear is so much different than having a 4-speed with a 4.10 gear or even the getrag 5-speed. granted that all the mods that you've done on your car and you have a 4-speed or 5-speed fiero...I bet ya..you'll notice a difference even with headers...so not making any war here...it's really hard to justify speed acceleration with automatic vs. a stick shift..unless you have a V8 engine in it...Also...WKDFIRO doesn't work with FOCOA...but I know that FOCOA's headers is better than any headers I've seen...Like I've said before...the guy who design this headers also builds headers for the Buick Grand Nationals...and he is not a Bull Shiter!!!! he takes his products seriously because of what's expected from him...||||reg||1
